`Vitest vi.mock() syntax ESM import() vs string module ID`

💡 Result:

Short answer — both forms work; use import(...) (a Promise) when you want TypeScript-aware, path-checked, or relative/aliased ESM imports and to get proper typing for importOriginal; use a string module ID when you want to refer to a module by its resolved ID (aliases, package name, or virtual ID). Vitest’s vi.mock overload accepts either a string path or a Promise returned by import(...). Examples and details:

- Promise (dynamic import) form — preferred for ESM/TS because the import(...) is type-checked by TypeScript and Vitest’s transformer strips the dynamic import and hoists the mock before the real import. You can also use the factory’s importOriginal to call the real module:
  - vi.mock(import('./myModule'), () => ({ fn: vi.fn() }))
  - vi.mock(import('vscode'), () => ({ window: { createOutputChannel: vi.fn() } }))
  - Docs: Vitest guide and API show this usage and explain hoisting/transform. [1][2]

- String module ID form — use when you want to mock by module ID (package name, aliased id, virtual id) or when you already refer to the module as a string:
  - vi.mock('./myModule')      // auto-mocks or loads __mocks__ if present
  - vi.mock('axios', () => ({ get: vi.fn() }))
  - Useful for jest-like __mocks__ lookup or when you don’t need import typings. [1][2]

Important notes:
- vi.mock is hoisted to the top of the file; it runs before imports. [2]
- It only works for modules imported with import (not require). [2]
- If you rely on setup files or cached imports, mocking may behave unexpectedly — prefer calling vi.mock in the test file (or use vi.doMock if you need non-hoisted behavior). [2]
- For builtins or node: prefixed names, mock the same ID you import in code (e.g., 'node:fs' vs 'fs') to match resolution. [1]

Sources:
[1] Vitest — Mocking Modules guide.  
[2] Vitest — vi API (vi.mock overloads and notes).

